A||||15||XDrewX||How many people with V8's have their battery in the trunk?||||1||
Z||000000||XDrewX||12-04-2001||04:32 PM||||I was just wondering if there is a real reason to put it up front...I dont use my trunk.<P>The only thing I could think, is so you could put your old tire in the trunk if you have a flat. If I remember correctly, A stock rim and tire wont fit where the emergency "donut" spare resides.<P><BR>Thanks<P>Andrew||||reg||1
Z||000001||JasonS||12-04-2001||04:47 PM||||Mine's in the trunk.  I didn't feel like paying the money to buy long battery cables.  Haven't had any problems with my setup.  Bought a marine battery box and riveted it to the left upper side of the trunk.<P>Jason||||reg||14
Z||000002||Tina||12-04-2001||06:36 PM||||Well, it's been in the trunk for the last two.<BR>But will move to the front this winter.<BR>(Not because I had any problems with it, just need all the trunk space I can get.)<P>Tina||||reg||1
Z||000003||West Coast Fiero||12-04-2001||06:42 PM||||you may notice that there is a factory instructional sticker underneath the front decklid ( pass side ) explaining that a full size factory wheel is meant to be placed in the trunk in the event of a flat tire.<P> V-8's or not, most choose to put the battery in the front compartment for increased weight transfer, and with most designs offered by Fiero aftermarket companies, the stock spare tire will fit perfectly over the front battery after relocation<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000004||XDrewX||12-04-2001||08:23 PM||||Well Mr. West Coast, <P>If I ordered your batt box, how long would it take you to get it to me?<BR>Now remember I am on the East Coast.<P>Thanks,<BR>Andrew||||reg||1
Z||000005||West Coast Fiero||12-04-2001||09:20 PM||||we use FedEx 2 day air, thus it would take two days to get to you once the item is made. I beleive I have one battery box in stock and the cables for the box are made on the same day as the order. One day when I have the time, when not spinning wrenches on customer's cars, doing the Fieros West web page, and not dealing with suppliers - I will spend the time to build up our normally stocking items again.<P>For a battery box it normally takes 1 day of fabrication, providing that I have Chris's full and undivided attention seeing as how at this time he is the only one here ( out of three employees ) that can weld aluminum.<P>so to answer your question upmost and fully, give us a call and I will tell you my best estimate in the amount of time from you placing the order to the items being on your door step<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000006||Bazooka||12-04-2001||11:00 PM||||DrewX,   Front mount battery installations are Junk. They are a Royal Pain In The Ass when servicing the electrical system. Especially when disconnecting and reconnecting the terminals. It is true that the battery will fit in there under the spare tire, But you really have to fight with it to get it out each time you need to cut off the juice. I put in a remote cutoff switch to help. Also, when you move the 40 pound battery to the front, you actually are doubling the weight that you are pushing in straight line acceleration.<BR> Don't waste your money, just put the battery in the back trunk.<P>****************************** <A HREF="http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html" TARGET=_blank>http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html</A> <BR>******************************||||reg||1
Z||000007||WKDFIRO||12-04-2001||11:09 PM||||Waiting for California Kid to respond to see if his battery is still in the engine compartment...it doesn't need to be in the trunk.||||reg||2
Z||000008||artherd||12-04-2001||11:21 PM||||WHAT THE?!<P>Um. no.  Just flat out no. Doubling the weight?  Are we all insane?!<P>In stock V6 cars, relocating the battery is one of the first things anyone serious about their cars performance does.  It helps hugely with not only the front-rear weight balance, but in moving more weight from above, to *below* the car's center of mass.(aka CG)<P>PS: want a cheap source for looong battery cables?  #2gauge jumper cables, 19.95 :L)<P>[QUOTE]Originally posted by Bazooka:<BR>[B]Also, when you move the 40 pound battery to the front, you actually are doubling the weight that you are pushing in straight line acceleration.<BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000009||Butter||12-05-2001||11:32 AM||||Here's where I put mine  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Water_Pump_2.jpg"> <BR>I just relocated the battery more to the corner, made a surface out of stainless steel and put the stock "bottom battery block lock" on it.  <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/buttersig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||MinnGreenGT||12-05-2001||12:11 PM||||I agree with Artherd... moving it to the front is a performance improvement even on V6's!  If you're worried about accessing the battery, buy an optima battery- they are sealed and can be mounted at any angle! and just strap it on top of the spare.  I'd also invest in a set of quick disconnect fittings to easy installation and removal.<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Hartz||12-05-2001||02:37 PM||||Spare, shmare.  Buy a can of Fix-A-Flat and a AAA subscription!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/battery.JPG"> <P>Hartz<P>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo<BR>hartzfeld.home.mindspring.com||||reg||1
Z||000012||jelly2m8||12-05-2001||06:36 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Butter:<BR><B>Here's where I put mine    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Water_Pump_2.jpg">   <BR>I just relocated the battery more to the corner, made a surface out of stainless steel and put the stock "bottom battery block lock" on it.  <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What adapter kit are you using? Looks like more clearance than an Archie swap. But that does look like Archies alternator bracket.<P> Nice set-up!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by jelly2m8 (edited 12-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Spyder||12-06-2001||12:03 AM||||Move it to the Front...<BR>Why not go crazy and do what I did...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fuelcell.JPG"> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||California Kid||12-06-2001||02:13 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by WKDFIRO:<BR><B>Waiting for California Kid to respond to see if his battery is still in the engine compartment...it doesn't need to be in the trunk.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Yep....Still in the engine compartment, very nicely packaged on the left hand side, yes I said left hand side.  The guys at FieroMotorsports (in '93) who performed the professional V8 engine conversion, fabricated a battery tray and located it off the firewall over in the corner where the V6 air cleaner would be. Don't have pictures to post, but it's very clean.  Don't have figures but I think it balances the weight on the rear tires as rear end reaction is the same in either sharp left or right turns.<P>Ben (artherd) has a point about the up front location, it would place the mass in a lower center of gravity. I had considered moving mine to that location, but I couldn't find enough reasons to do it, as I'm getting very good G numbers now. Final note, I haven't put a battery on a scale, but I'm guessing they are more like about 20-25 pounds, not 40.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Butter||12-06-2001||10:07 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jelly2m8:<BR><B>  What adapter kit are you using? Looks like more clearance than an Archie swap. But that does look like Archies alternator bracket.<P> Nice set-up!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I used an Archie economy kit and come up with the incidentals on my own.  The original battery tray was in poor shape so I just fabbed one of my own.  I think for balance the battery would be better up front but I elected to keep the battery cables short and installation easy.<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/buttersig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
